# {{ projectName }}

Built with the **web** starter kit — the full framework: JSX views rendered on
the server, Tailwind v4, cookie sessions, session authentication and Drizzle.

## Run it

```bash
deno task css:build   # once, to produce public/css/app.css
deno task dev         # http://localhost:8888
deno task test        # the smoke tests — no database needed
```

Two terminals during development, so the stylesheet rebuilds as you edit:

```bash
deno task css:watch   # terminal 1
deno task dev         # terminal 2
```

## Add the database

The scaffold boots without one on purpose: `DATABASE_URL` is commented out in
`.env`, so the kernel skips the connection instead of printing a failure on
every reload. When you are ready:

```bash
# .env
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/{{ projectName }}
```

```bash
deno task db:migrate
```

The migration was generated from `app/model/user.ts`. Change a model, then
`deno task cli db:generate` writes the next one.

Then create a user. The `password` column holds a **hash** — never a password:

```ts
import { hashPassword } from '@lockness/auth'
await db.insert(users).values({
    email: 'you@example.com',
    password: await hashPassword('correct horse battery staple'),
})
```

Sign in at `/auth/login`; `/auth/profile` is the page that requires it.

## What is here

```text
app/
├── auth/
│   ├── guards.ts         # the 'web' SessionGuard
│   └── user_provider.ts  # the two lookups the provider needs
├── controller/
│   ├── app_controller.tsx   # GET /
│   └── auth_controller.tsx  # login, logout, profile
├── model/user.ts
├── view/
│   ├── app.ts            # the renderer
│   ├── assets/app.css    # Tailwind entry point
│   ├── components/ui.tsx
│   ├── layouts/main_layout.tsx
│   └── pages/{home,login}.tsx
├── kernel.ts             # session, auth, database, cache
└── routes.ts             # explicit registry, for production
config/                   # app, cache, compile, database, routing, session, listeners
database/migrations/      # generated from app/model by drizzle-kit
├── 0000_create_users.sql
└── meta/                 # journal + snapshot — db:migrate reads them
drizzle.config.ts         # what db:generate, db:migrate and db:fresh read
public/                   # static files, compiled CSS, favicons
tests/smoke.test.ts
```

## The cache

`app/kernel.ts` sets `cache: config.cache`, and `deno.json` declares
`@lockness/cache` for it. `config/cache.ts` picks the memory driver in
development and Deno KV in production — which is why `deno.json` carries
`"unstable": ["kv"]`; without it the KV driver throws on first use. On Deno
Deploy, leave `DATABASE_KV_PATH` unset to use the platform's database.

Every optional feature the kernel names must be a package `deno.json`
declares: drop `@lockness/cache` but keep the `cache` key and the app refuses
to boot with `MissingOptionalPackageError`, naming the package and the fix.
To go without a cache, remove the key from `app/kernel.ts` and `cache` from
`config/mod.ts`.

## The two things worth knowing

**Middleware order is load-bearing.** In `app/kernel.ts` the session comes
before the authenticator. Swap them and every request is anonymous, with
nothing to say why.

**The guard is called `web` for a reason.** `@AuthRequired()` with no argument
looks for a guard literally named `web` — it does not read `authConfig.default`.
Rename it and every bare `@AuthRequired()` stops working; pass the name
explicitly (`@AuthRequired('admin')`) when you add others.

## Tailwind v4

`app/view/assets/app.css` is the entry point. It imports Tailwind, and
`deno task css:build` compiles it with the Tailwind CLI into
`public/css/app.css`, the stylesheet `app/view/layouts/main_layout.tsx` links.
`css:watch` runs the same command and rebuilds on every change. Tailwind scans
`app/` for class names, so a class written anywhere there gets a rule.
`tailwindcss` and `@tailwindcss/cli` come from npm: Tailwind is not on JSR.

The entry file declares design tokens as CSS variables in `:root`, and exposes
them as theme colours in `@theme inline` — that is what makes `bg-primary` or
`text-muted-foreground` exist. Put base styles inside `@layer base { … }`: an
unlayered rule outranks every utility, whatever its specificity.

CSS variables use **parentheses**, arbitrary literals use brackets:

```html
<div class="bg-(--primary) px-[0.75rem]">
```

`bg-[--primary]` is the v3 spelling and silently does nothing in v4.

## What is deliberately missing

Email, queues, storage and the scheduler are not wired in. Each is one package
and three steps — install it, add a `config/` module, name it in `@Kernel`.

Want less? Two lighter kits:

```bash
deno run -A jsr:@lockness/init my-api --kit=api   # JSON API, tokens, OpenAPI
deno run -A jsr:@lockness/init my-app --kit=slim  # one controller, nothing else
```

## Commands

```bash
deno task cli make:controller Product
deno task cli make:model Product -a
deno task cli make:middleware Admin
deno task cli router:list
deno task cli db:migrate
deno task routes:generate
```

